<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jahriyya revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1781">1781</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jahriyya revolt of 1781 was a rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> that grew out of sectarian violence between two Naqshbandi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> orders, the Jahriyya and their rivals the Khafiyya. After street fighting and lawsuits between them, the Qing arrested the Jahriyya leader Ma Mingxin; his followers tried to free him by force, which led to his execution and the crushing of the rebels with the help of the Khafiyya and Muslim loyalists. Salar and other Jahriyya rebels, labelled the New Teaching, were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guizhou">Guizhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>. The Dungan Revolt of 1895–96 broke out in the same place for very similar reasons.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of the banner garrisons there, and Mongol, Russian and Muslim convicts from Inner Asia were sent to the Han banner garrison at Guangzhou; after the <b>Jahriyya revolt</b> in Gansu was put down in the 1780s, Muslims such as Ma Jinlu were exiled there as slaves of banner officers.</blockquote><a href="/societies/qing-china">Social Hierarchy of Qing China</a></li></ul></section>
